      <description>Peter Drucker did more than anybody to turn management into a profession. He praised managers not just as engines of economic progress but as guardians of the liberal order. “Management is the alternative to tyranny,” he once said, speaking with the authority of somebody who fled his native Vienna in the 1930s, “and our only guarantee against it.” He also turned management into an exciting intellectual discipline, discovering compelling ideas in Japan as well as America, the voluntary sector as</description>
